<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Mycale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">479 BCE</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Mycale was fought in 479 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a>. A Greek force under the Spartan king Leotychidas and the Athenian Xanthippus landed and destroyed a Persian army and its beached fleet, traditionally on the same day as the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. It ended the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> and opened the liberation of Ionia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mycale.jpg" alt="Battle of Mycale" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Pedro Lassouras assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priene_colline_colonne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
